# Second humanizer pass: stricter rewrite of prose paragraphs. Numbers, citations and caveats are unchanged.
from content import *
import content as _c
NEW = {
"Silicon sampling asks a language model to answer a survey question":
 "Silicon sampling asks a language model to answer a survey question as a respondent with a given demographic profile would. If it worked, it would be a cheap complement to polling [@argyle2023]. So far the evidence is mixed. Models reproduce demographic-attitude correlations in US data [@argyle2023] but misrepresent many groups [@santurkar2023]. At the individual level they match or trail statistical baselines trained on the same demographics [@chen2026,@miranda2025], over-attribute attitudes to demographics and inflate gaps between groups [@chen2026], and persona variables explain little of the real variance [@hu2024].",
"We study these questions for India":
 "We study these questions in India, using Pew's Religion in India survey and wave 7 of the World Values Survey. Four controls separate the profile's effect from the model's prior: a prompt with no profile, a label-free prior correction, a fine-tuned copy of the same model, and a test on held-out items. The questions are: how a prompted LLM uses the profile (RQ1); what fine-tuning changes relative to the zero-shot model and to matched classifiers (RQ2); whether the adaptation transfers to unseen questions (RQ3); and whether a larger zero-shot model or more labelled respondents change the picture (RQ4).",
"Our contributions are:":
 "We contribute (i) profile-use diagnostics that separate accuracy from mechanism: a profile ablation, the demographic variance share, and the directional validity of group differences; (ii) evidence from India that a zero-shot 7B model uses the profile heavily but with weak direction, while fine-tuning on respondent-level data recovers most of the gap to matched classifiers; (iii) a held-out-item test, reported with its limits, in which the adapter shows no detectable gain on unseen questions; and (iv) checks on model scale, on how many labelled respondents classical models need, and a WVS-7 replication.",
"**Fine-tuned model.**":
 "**Fine-tuned model.** We used QLoRA [@dettmers2023,@hu2022]: 4-bit NF4 weights, rank 16, alpha 32, dropout 0.05 on the q/k/v/o projections (10.1M trainable parameters). Training ran for one epoch over 184,853 examples from 23,999 respondents, with effective batch 32, peak learning rate 2e-4, seed 42 and loss over the whole sequence, and took 6.6 hours on one GPU. Predictions are the argmax of the next-token distribution over the option digits after the training-format prompt, scored on a Kaggle T4 in fp16. We use the same readout for every zero-shot condition. The original run decoded greedily and ended the prompt without the separator; that readout scores 36.1% and agrees with ours on 71.7% of rows. We report both and treat the training-format readout as primary. The transfer test in Section IV-C used an MLX re-implementation of the adapter on a 4-bit MLX base, which is only approximate.",
"**Diagnostics.**":
 "**Diagnostics.** All use answer indices normalised to [0, 1] on ordinal items. The *demographic variance share* is the increase in R-squared when profile dummies are added to item fixed effects, computed for predictions and for true answers; it parallels the variance-explained index in [@chen2026]. *Directional validity* works on (item, axis, category) cells with enough respondents (15 on the subset, 100 on the full Pew fold, 8 on WVS-7). For each cell we take the category mean minus the item mean, for predictions and for the truth, and report the Pearson correlation r between the two and the slope of predicted on true (below 1 is attenuation, above 1 inflation). Intervals resample respondents. Resampling attenuates cell means, so the intervals sit below the point estimates and show uncertainty, not exact coverage. The variance share uses probability-weighted predictions for the zero-shot model and hard predictions for the fine-tuned model and GBM. *Distributional fidelity* is the Jensen-Shannon divergence (base 2) between the mean predicted probability vector and the empirical answer distribution in each (item, subgroup) cell with at least 40 respondents, averaged over cells. We also report exact-match accuracy, negative log-likelihood (NLL), expected calibration error (ECE), paired respondent-bootstrap differences, and Holm-corrected permutation tests for subgroup accuracy gaps (best minus worst category).",
"Table I summarises the zero-shot behaviour.":
 "Table I summarises the zero-shot behaviour. With the chat template the model scores 17.5% (95% CI 15.7 to 19.4), 12.7 points below the majority class and at chance (19.4%). The raw prompt does the same (18.8%, 120 respondents). Removing the profile raises accuracy to 21.1%, so the profile changes accuracy by -3.6 points (-5.9 to -1.2). The model still reacts strongly to it: its prediction changes on 50% of rows relative to no profile, and the profile explains 29% of its prediction variance, against 4% for real answers and 6% for GBM. Its group differences are weakly aligned with the truth (r = 0.22, 95% CI 0.02 to 0.35) and strongly attenuated (slope 0.18), as Fig. 1 shows. On the full fold (6,000 respondents, 46,209 rows) the same model scores 16.1% with the chat template (95% CI 15.7 to 16.4) and 18.0% with the raw prompt, against 19.4% for chance and 30.4% for the majority class, so the subset result is not a sampling artefact. A prior correction recovers about two thirds of the distance to the majority class (26.3%) and no more. The model's NLL (3.88) is worse than guessing uniformly, with probability concentrated on one option for most respondents (mean top-option share 0.71).",
"On the full fold (Table II)":
 "On the full fold (Table II) the fine-tuned model reaches 38.0% (95% CI 37.5 to 38.4). That is 7.6 points above the majority class, 0.5 below logistic regression (-0.8 to -0.2) and 2.1 below GBM, so it closes 79% (75 to 83) of the gap between the majority class and GBM. Its NLL of 1.48 sits between logistic regression (1.47) and the majority class (1.59) and above GBM (1.44), and it is well calibrated (ECE 0.009). It trails GBM on every item, by 1.2 to 2.7 points (Table III). Averaging its probabilities with GBM's gives no accuracy gain over GBM (-0.2 points, 95% CI -0.5 to 0.0). The original run's decoding produced a constant answer on one item (Q12). Under the training-format readout the model predicts the majority answer for most respondents there and its mean probabilities follow the true shares, so the collapse came from the decoding, not the adapter.",
"On the 200-respondent subset the fine-tuned model":
 "On the 200-respondent subset the fine-tuned model is 20.2 points above the zero-shot model (paired 95% CI 16.9 to 23.5) and 11.4 points above the prior-corrected one. Its group differences correlate r = 0.58 with the truth on the subset (GBM 0.64) and 0.82 on the full fold (GBM 0.91). The slope is near one on the subset (1.07) and above one on the full fold (1.87, GBM 1.85). The two use different cell thresholds (15 and 100 respondents), so they are not directly comparable. Hard predictions inflate between-group differences for all three methods (2.0 times for the fine-tuned model, 2.0 for GBM, 2.6 for logistic regression). That is consistent with argmax predictions inflating group differences in general, though we did not test soft predictions.",
"The fine-tuned model's subgroup gaps":
 "The fine-tuned model's subgroup gaps (Table IV) differ from zero on five of eight axes after Holm correction: religion 10.0 points, region 8.8, age 2.7, income 1.7 and urban/rural 1.3. They differ from GBM's by at most 1.9 points, nominally only for region (+1.4, CI 0.1 to 3.5, uncorrected). On probabilities, its predicted subgroup distributions are close to the truth. The mean Jensen-Shannon divergence is 0.009 across religion cells and 0.006 across region cells, against 0.004 and 0.002 for logistic regression, 0.029 and 0.020 for the item marginal, and 0.271 and 0.293 for the zero-shot model. So the fine-tuned model is two to three times further from the truth than the classifiers on religion and region, and more than ten times closer than the zero-shot model. Tuning the classifiers on a validation split of the training respondents changes little (logistic 38.5%, GBM 40.2%), so the gap to GBM is not due to under-tuned classifiers. The fine-tuned configuration itself was not tuned.",
"We applied the adapter to eight Pew items":
 "We applied the adapter to eight Pew items it never saw. On the trained items the MLX re-implementation scores 32.9% against 18.8% for its base (a gain of 14.1 points), and the original model scores 40.5%. The port therefore reproduces much of the effect but is weaker; row agreement is 56% and directional validity is similar (0.71 against 0.63 for the original). On the unseen items (Table V, Fig. 2) the adapter scores 28.9% and its base 28.5%, a difference of +0.4 points (-5.1 to +5.9), against +14.1 in domain. Over respondents the in-domain gain exceeds the unseen gain by 13.7 points (95% CI 7.0 to 20.4), but that interval treats the eight items on each side as fixed. Across items the difference is 14.0 points with a standard error of 10.2 (permutation p = 0.18), and the unseen gain runs from -21 to +36 points per item. We therefore do not claim the in-domain gain is reliably larger. The safe reading is that no gain on unseen items is detectable. Two weaker signals remain: calibration (NLL 2.39 to 1.69, still above the uniform value of 1.65) and the direction of group differences (r = 0.24, 95% CI 0.03 to 0.35, against -0.02 for the base). Classifiers fitted with labels on these items reach 37.6% (majority), 44.7% and 47.6%, so here the adapted LLM is no substitute for data on the new question. The test rests on the approximate port, 120 respondents, eight items and one narrow training set, so it does not show that broader fine-tuning would also fail to transfer.",
"**Larger zero-shot model.** gpt-oss-120b on 100":
 "**Larger zero-shot model.** gpt-oss-120b scores 27.6% (24.9 to 30.3) on 100 respondents (774 rows). That is 2.3 points below the majority class (30.0%, not significant), against 18.7% for the 7B model on the same rows, and 11.8 points below the fine-tuned 7B model (7.1 to 16.5). It is a different, larger model and we did not vary scale within one family, so we cannot credit the difference from the 7B model to scale. It reaches the majority class and goes no further.",
"**Labelled data.**":
 "**Labelled data.** Classical models need a fair amount of labelled data before they beat the majority class. Averaged over five random subsets, logistic regression matches the majority class up to N = 100 respondents (30.5% against 29.1%), beats it by 3.9 points at N = 400 (33.7%) and by 6.4 at N = 1,600 (36.4%). GBM clears the majority class in accuracy only at about 1,600 respondents (34.6%), and its NLL is still worse than the majority class's there (1.69 against 1.59). On the same 200 test respondents, the fine-tuned 7B model, trained on 23,999 respondents, scores 37.7%, slightly above logistic regression trained on 1,600 (36.4%); the gap is within the sampling error of 200 respondents. Whether LLM adaptation is more sample-efficient than classical models with little data is the open question most likely to favour LLMs. We could not test it reliably with 7B models on our hardware, and a small-model (0.5B) attempt on up to 100 respondents collapsed to a near-constant answer, so we draw no conclusion from it.",
"Across the 144 screened items":
 "Across the 144 screened items the majority class reaches 45.5% and the cell mode 45.4%, against 46.9% for logistic regression and 50.1% for GBM, so demographics add only 1.4 and 4.6 points. On the 15 evaluated items (Table VI), Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite (30 respondents) scores 25.7% and gpt-oss-120b (40) scores 23.0%. Both are within sampling error of the majority class and 9.5 and 11.3 points below GBM. Zero-shot Qwen2.5-7B (120 respondents, 1,716 rows) scores 22.1%, 2.7 points below the majority class (95% CI -5.6 to +0.1) and 15.3 below GBM. Its group differences show no directional validity (r = -0.03 over 345 cells; GBM 0.64), the profile changes accuracy by -1.3 points (-4.2 to +1.5), and on seven of the 15 items (nine at a 90% threshold) it gives the same answer to nearly every respondent. The 15 items come from an earlier run and have their own majority class of 21 to 25%, well below the 144-item figure, so each LLM is compared with the majority class on the same rows. Subgroup gaps cannot be told apart from random group assignment in any of 12 API-model tests (11 of 12 with p > 0.05, none after Holm correction) or in the 120-respondent run (smallest p = 0.082). Detection thresholds range from 4.5 to 26.7 points, above most gaps seen on Pew (1 to 2.5 points).",
"**What the profile does.**":
 "**What the profile does.** A zero-shot model does not ignore the profile. It varies with it far more than real answers do, in directions that match the data only weakly, and the net effect on exact-match accuracy is slightly negative. That fits small, noisy persona effects [@hu2024] and the over-determination reported in [@chen2026]. We did not vary prompts, so we cannot say whether richer or better-worded demographics would help.",
"**What fine-tuning does.**":
 "**What fine-tuning does.** Fine-tuning on respondent-level data gets item marginals and group differences close to a classifier's, but not all the way. Accuracy ends 2.1 points below GBM and 0.5 below logistic regression, directional validity on the full fold is 0.82 against 0.91, and the two models agree on 72% of predictions. The absence of a detectable gain on unseen items fits an item-specific mapping from profile to answer. Given the port, the number of items and the narrow training set, it is not evidence that transfer is impossible.",
"**Where LLM respondents could add value.**":
 "**Where LLM respondents could add value.** On this evidence, not as substitutes for labelled data on the same question, since matched classifiers are cheaper and more accurate. Nor, at 7B with narrow fine-tuning, as predictors for new questions. The open settings are the ones our design cannot test: richer text-conditioned personas, much broader fine-tuning across many items, and aggregate distribution estimates from probabilities rather than argmax.",
"Results come from one fold":
 "Everything comes from one fold, one seed and one epoch. Scoring used fp16 on a T4 while training used bf16, and the original run's greedy decoding of the same adapter scores lower (36.1%), so results depend on the readout. We use the one that matches training and the zero-shot conditions. Fine-tuning used eight religion- and fertility-related items and a full-sequence loss, so transfer to broader item sets is untested and the unseen-item null may reflect narrow training. The transfer and probability results use an MLX re-implementation of the adapter that is about 7 to 8 points weaker than the original on the same rows. The transfer comparison is therefore conservative for the original model, and it rests on eight items and 120 respondents with large item-level uncertainty. The local zero-shot controls use random subsets of 100 to 200 respondents, though the full-fold zero-shot result agrees. We tested one 7B model locally and two free-tier API models and did not test frontier models on these surveys (Chen et al. [@chen2026] did, on US and cross-national data, and saw the same failures). We used two surveys with eight and 15 items and no language axis. Pew survey weights are not applied, so statements describe the sample, not the population. Accuracy is exact match on ordinal items with argmax predictions, directional-validity cells share respondents across axes, and the variance share is an in-sample increase in R-squared.",
"A prompted 7B model varies strongly":
 "A prompted 7B model varies strongly with a demographic profile but uses it weakly and slightly harmfully. Fine-tuning on respondent-level data recovers most, though not all, of the accuracy and direction gap to classifiers on the trained questions, and shows no detectable accuracy gain on eight new ones. In our settings matched classifiers remain the benchmark LLM respondents have to beat, and held-out questions at larger scale are the experiment most likely to change that. Code, prompts and analysis scripts are at github.com/DevilHunter695/silicon-sampling-capstone. WVS-7 must be obtained from the WVS Association and the Pew data from Pew Research Center.",
}
